(function(m,c){var L=window.ACADEMY.lessons;for(var id in c){Object.assign(L[id],c[id]);}(window.ACADEMY_LOADED=window.ACADEMY_LOADED||{})[m]=1;})("argocd",{"argocd/pull-based-delivery-and-the-repository-as-source-of-truth":{"html":"\u003cp>In push-based delivery a pipeline holds cluster credentials and runs kubectl or helm against the cluster. In pull-based delivery a controller inside the cluster watches a repository and makes the cluster match what is stored there. The second approach changes who holds credentials, how drift is handled and how you audit changes. Argo CD is the most widely used implementation, and this lesson explains the idea before the tool.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Contrast push and pull delivery\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Explain the repository as the source of truth\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Describe reconciliation and drift\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Know when this model fits and when it does not\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"push-and-pull\">Push and pull\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Push:\u003c/strong> a CI job builds an image, then runs \u003ccode>kubectl apply\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>helm upgrade\u003c/code> using credentials for the cluster. The job is the only place that knows the result, and credentials must be stored in the CI system. \u003cstrong>Pull:\u003c/strong> CI builds the image and commits a change to a configuration repository (for example, a new image tag). A controller in the cluster notices the commit and applies it. The cluster reaches out; nothing needs inbound access or stored cluster credentials in CI.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-repository-as-source-of-truth\">The repository as source of truth\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The desired state of the cluster is written as manifests, Helm charts or Kustomize overlays in a repository. A change is a commit, reviewed in a pull request, with an author, a message and a history. Rolling back is reverting a commit. The history answers &quot;who changed this, when and why?&quot; without searching through pipeline logs.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"reconciliation-and-drift\">Reconciliation and drift\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The controller continuously compares \u003cstrong>desired state\u003c/strong> (the repository) with \u003cstrong>live state\u003c/strong> (the cluster). If someone edits a resource by hand, the two diverge: that is \u003cstrong>drift\u003c/strong>. The controller can report it, or put things back. This turns manual hot-fixes from invisible changes into visible differences.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"trade-offs\">Trade-offs\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Benefits: audit trail, consistent environments, simple rollback, fewer credentials in CI, and recovery by pointing a new cluster at the repository. Costs: a separate configuration repository to manage, a delay between commit and rollout, secrets need special handling because the repository must not contain them in plain text, and more concepts for newcomers. It fits Kubernetes well; it fits less well for one-off scripts or resources outside the cluster.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> Someone edits a Deployment by hand in production. With a pull-based controller using automatic self-healing, what happens?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> The controller detects drift and reverts the change to match the repository.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The repository wins.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-hot-fix-that-vanished\">Incident: The hot-fix that vanished\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>An engineer scaled a deployment up by hand during a spike, and twenty minutes later it returned to 2 replicas.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl scale deploy/api --replicas=10 -n shop\nkubectl get deploy api -n shop\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl scale deploy/api --replicas=10 -n shop\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get deploy api -n shop\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">NAME  READY  UP-TO-DATE\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">api   2/2    2\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Automatic self-healing restored the repository value. The team learned to change replicas by commit or by autoscaler, and agreed on an emergency procedure: pause automation for that application, fix, then commit the same change.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Treating the repository as optional\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Putting plain-text secrets in it\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Hand-editing the cluster and expecting it to stay\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Mixing application code and cluster configuration without a plan\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"push-and-pull","text":"Push and pull"},{"id":"the-repository-as-source-of-truth","text":"The repository as source of truth"},{"id":"reconciliation-and-drift","text":"Reconciliation and drift"},{"id":"trade-offs","text":"Trade-offs"},{"id":"incident-the-hot-fix-that-vanished","text":"Incident: The hot-fix that vanished"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Argo CD core concepts","u":"https://argo-cd.readthedocs.io/en/stable/core_concepts/"},{"t":"Argo CD documentation","u":"https://argo-cd.readthedocs.io/en/stable/"}],"text":"In push-based delivery a pipeline holds cluster credentials and runs kubectl or helm against the cluster. In pull-based delivery a controller inside the cluster watches a repository and makes the cluster match what is stored there. The second approach changes who holds credentials, how drift is handled and how you audit changes. Argo CD is the most widely used implementation, and this lesson explains the idea before the tool. What you will learn Contrast push and pull delivery Explain the repository as the source of truth Describe reconciliation and drift Know when this model fits and when it does not Push and pull Push: a CI job builds an image, then runs kubectl apply or helm upgrade using credentials for the cluster. The job is the only place that knows the result, and credentials must be stored in the CI system. Pull: CI builds the image and commits a change to a configuration repository (for example, a new image tag). A controller in the cluster notices the commit and applies it. The cluster reaches out; nothing needs inbound access or stored cluster credentials in CI. The repository as source of truth The desired state of the cluster is written as manifests, Helm charts or Kustomize overlays in a repository. A change is a commit, reviewed in a pull request, with an author, a message and a history. Rolling back is reverting a commit. The history answers \"who changed this, when and why?\" without searching through pipeline logs. Reconciliation and drift The controller continuously compares desired state (the repository) with live state (the cluster). If someone edits a resource by hand, the two diverge: that is drift . The controller can report it, or put things back. This turns manual hot-fixes from invisible changes into visible differences. Trade-offs Benefits: audit trail, consistent environments, simple rollback, fewer credentials in CI, and recovery by pointing a new cluster at the repository. Costs: a separate configuration repository to manage, a delay between commit and rollout, secrets need special handling because the repository must not contain them in plain text, and more concepts for newcomers. It fits Kubernetes well; it fits less well for one-off scripts or resources outside the cluster. Predict first. Someone edits a Deployment by hand in production. With a pull-based controller using automatic self-healing, what happens? Your guess Answer: The controller detects drift and reverts the change to match the repository. The repository wins. Incident: The hot-fix that vanished Incident An engineer scaled a deployment up by hand during a spike, and twenty minutes later it returned to 2 replicas. $ kubectl scale deploy/api --replicas=10 -n shop $ kubectl get deploy api -n shop NAME READY UP-TO-DATE api 2/2 2 Automatic self-healing restored the repository value. The team learned to change replicas by commit or by autoscaler, and agreed on an emergency procedure: pause automation for that application, fix, then commit the same change. Common mistakes Treating the repository as optional Putting plain-text secrets in it Hand-editing the cluster and expecting it to stay Mixing application code and cluster configuration without a plan"},"argocd/installing-argo-cd-and-the-application-resource":{"html":"\u003cp>Argo CD runs in your cluster as a set of components: an API server and UI, a repository server that renders manifests, and an application controller that compares and syncs. You tell it what to deliver with a custom resource called an Application, which names a source in a repository and a destination in a cluster.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Install Argo CD and reach its UI\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Name the main components\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Write an Application manifest\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Understand source, destination and project\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"install\">Install\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Create a namespace and apply the official install manifest (or use the Helm chart):\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl create namespace argocd\nkubectl apply -n argocd -f install.yaml   # the stable install manifest from the Getting started page\nkubectl -n argocd get pods\nkubectl -n argocd get secret argocd-initial-admin-secret -o jsonpath=&quot;{.data.password}&quot; | base64 -d\nkubectl -n argocd port-forward svc/argocd-server 8080:443\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">kubectl create namespace argocd\nkubectl apply -n argocd -f install.yaml   \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># the stable install manifest from the Getting started page\u003c/span>\nkubectl -n argocd get pods\nkubectl -n argocd get secret argocd-initial-admin-secret -o jsonpath=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;{.data.password}&quot;\u003c/span> | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">base64\u003c/span> -d\nkubectl -n argocd port-forward svc/argocd-server 8080:443\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The initial admin password is in a secret; change it or switch to single sign-on and delete the secret. For production, pin a version, run it highly available and put the UI behind TLS and an ingress.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"components\">Components\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The \u003cstrong>API server\u003c/strong> serves the UI, CLI and API and handles authentication. The \u003cstrong>repo server\u003c/strong> clones repositories and renders manifests from plain YAML, Helm or Kustomize. The \u003cstrong>application controller\u003c/strong> compares the rendered desired state with live state and performs syncs. Redis caches state and optional components handle notifications and ApplicationSets.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-application-resource\">The Application resource\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: argoproj.io/v1alpha1\nkind: Application\nmetadata:\n  name: shop-api\n  namespace: argocd\nspec:\n  project: default\n  source:\n    repoURL: https://example.com/platform/shop-config.git\n    targetRevision: main\n    path: apps/shop-api\n  destination:\n    server: https://kubernetes.default.svc\n    namespace: shop\n  syncPolicy:\n    syncOptions:\n      - CreateNamespace=true\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">argoproj.io/v1alpha1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Application\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop-api\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">namespace:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">argocd\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">project:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">default\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">source:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">repoURL:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">https://example.com/platform/shop-config.git\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">targetRevision:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">main\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">path:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">apps/shop-api\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">destination:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">server:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">https://kubernetes.default.svc\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">namespace:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">syncPolicy:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">syncOptions:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">CreateNamespace=true\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The \u003cstrong>source\u003c/strong> says which repository, revision and folder. The \u003cstrong>destination\u003c/strong> says which cluster and namespace. The \u003cstrong>project\u003c/strong> groups applications and limits what they may do. With no automated sync policy, you press Sync (or run \u003ccode>argocd app sync\u003c/code>) yourself.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"cli-and-ui\">CLI and UI\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The CLI logs in with \u003ccode>argocd login\u003c/code>, lists applications with \u003ccode>argocd app list\u003c/code> and shows differences with \u003ccode>argocd app diff shop-api\u003c/code>. The UI displays the resource tree, sync and health status, and events. Both use the same API and the same permissions.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> You create an Application but nothing appears in the shop namespace. What did you probably forget?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> To sync it: without an automated policy the application stays OutOfSync until someone syncs.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Manual by default.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-unknown-repository\">Incident: Unknown repository\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A new application showed &quot;ComparisonError: repository not accessible&quot;.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"argocd app get shop-api | head -8\nargocd repo list\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>argocd app get shop-api | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -8\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Sync Status: Unknown\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Condition: ComparisonError: rpc error: authentication required\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>argocd repo list\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">TYPE  NAME  REPO  STATUS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(none)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The repository was private and had not been registered. The team added it with a read-only deploy credential through the repository settings, and the application compared successfully. They kept credentials in the cluster as secrets and not in the Application manifest.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Leaving the initial admin password in place\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Running the default project with no limits\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using a write-capable credential to read a repository\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Installing without pinning a version\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"install","text":"Install"},{"id":"components","text":"Components"},{"id":"the-application-resource","text":"The Application resource"},{"id":"cli-and-ui","text":"CLI and UI"},{"id":"incident-unknown-repository","text":"Incident: Unknown repository"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Argo CD getting started","u":"https://argo-cd.readthedocs.io/en/stable/getting_started/"},{"t":"Argo CD core concepts","u":"https://argo-cd.readthedocs.io/en/stable/core_concepts/"}],"text":"Argo CD runs in your cluster as a set of components: an API server and UI, a repository server that renders manifests, and an application controller that compares and syncs. You tell it what to deliver with a custom resource called an Application, which names a source in a repository and a destination in a cluster. What you will learn Install Argo CD and reach its UI Name the main components Write an Application manifest Understand source, destination and project Install Create a namespace and apply the official install manifest (or use the Helm chart): kubectl create namespace argocd kubectl apply -n argocd -f install.yaml # the stable install manifest from the Getting started page kubectl -n argocd get pods kubectl -n argocd get secret argocd-initial-admin-secret -o jsonpath= \"{.data.password}\" | base64 -d kubectl -n argocd port-forward svc/argocd-server 8080:443 The initial admin password is in a secret; change it or switch to single sign-on and delete the secret. For production, pin a version, run it highly available and put the UI behind TLS and an ingress. Components The API server serves the UI, CLI and API and handles authentication. The repo server clones repositories and renders manifests from plain YAML, Helm or Kustomize. The application controller compares the rendered desired state with live state and performs syncs. Redis caches state and optional components handle notifications and ApplicationSets. The Application resource apiVersion: argoproj.io/v1alpha1 kind: Application metadata: name: shop-api namespace: argocd spec: project: default source: repoURL: https://example.com/platform/shop-config.git targetRevision: main path: apps/shop-api destination: server: https://kubernetes.default.svc namespace: shop syncPolicy: syncOptions: - CreateNamespace=true The source says which repository, revision and folder. The destination says which cluster and namespace. The project groups applications and limits what they may do. With no automated sync policy, you press Sync (or run argocd app sync ) yourself. CLI and UI The CLI logs in with argocd login , lists applications with argocd app list and shows differences with argocd app diff shop-api . The UI displays the resource tree, sync and health status, and events. Both use the same API and the same permissions. Predict first. You create an Application but nothing appears in the shop namespace. What did you probably forget? Your guess Answer: To sync it: without an automated policy the application stays OutOfSync until someone syncs. Manual by default. Incident: Unknown repository Incident A new application showed \"ComparisonError: repository not accessible\". $ argocd app get shop-api | head -8 Sync Status: Unknown Condition: ComparisonError: rpc error: authentication required $ argocd repo list TYPE NAME REPO STATUS (none) The repository was private and had not been registered. The team added it with a read-only deploy credential through the repository settings, and the application compared successfully. They kept credentials in the cluster as secrets and not in the Application manifest. Common mistakes Leaving the initial admin password in place Running the default project with no limits Using a write-capable credential to read a repository Installing without pinning a version"},"argocd/sync-health-and-drift":{"html":"\u003cp>Argo CD shows two separate statuses for every application. Sync status says whether live state matches the repository. Health status says whether the resources actually work. They are independent, and reading them together tells you what to do next. Automation options such as prune and self-heal are powerful and need care.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Read Synced, OutOfSync, Healthy, Progressing and Degraded\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Choose a sync policy\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Understand prune and self-heal\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Use sync options and ignore rules for expected differences\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"two-statuses\">Two statuses\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Sync status:\u003c/strong> Synced (live matches desired), OutOfSync (they differ) or Unknown (cannot compare). \u003cstrong>Health:\u003c/strong> Healthy, Progressing (rolling out), Degraded (failing), Suspended, Missing. An application can be Synced but Degraded: the right manifests are applied but the pods crash. It can be OutOfSync but Healthy: someone changed a replica count and the app still works.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"manual-or-automated\">Manual or automated\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>With manual sync, Argo CD shows differences and waits for a person or pipeline to sync. With \u003cstrong>automated\u003c/strong> sync, it applies new commits on its own. Two further switches matter. \u003cstrong>Prune\u003c/strong> deletes resources that are no longer in the repository. \u003cstrong>Self-heal\u003c/strong> reverts live changes made outside the repository.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"syncPolicy:\n  automated:\n    prune: true\n    selfHeal: true\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">syncPolicy:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">automated:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">prune:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">true\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">selfHeal:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">true\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Use automation for non-production first. For production, many teams automate but keep prune off until they trust their repository layout, or require a manual sync for risky applications.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"why-prune-needs-care\">Why prune needs care\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Prune removes anything Argo CD manages that disappears from the source. A renamed folder, a wrong path or an empty render can look like &quot;delete everything&quot;. Protect critical resources with the annotation \u003ccode>argocd.argoproj.io/sync-options: Prune=false\u003c/code>, and use sync windows or manual approval for sensitive applications.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"expected-differences\">Expected differences\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Controllers add fields, such as an autoscaler changing replicas or a webhook injecting certificates. Tell Argo CD to ignore them with \u003ccode>ignoreDifferences\u003c/code> for those paths, or the application will show OutOfSync forever. Sync options like \u003ccode>ServerSideApply=true\u003c/code> help when several controllers manage one object.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"retrying\">Retrying\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A sync can fail on a bad manifest. Use \u003ccode>retry\u003c/code> settings for transient problems, and read the sync result message for permanent ones. Argo CD records sync history so you can see what was applied at each revision.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> An app is Synced but Degraded. What does that tell you?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> The manifests match the repository, but the workload is failing; look at pod events and logs, not at the diff.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Separate questions.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-prune-that-removed-a-namespace\">Incident: The prune that removed a namespace\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>After a folder rename, an automated application with prune turned on deleted its resources.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"argocd app get shop-api --refresh | head -6\nargocd app diff shop-api | head -3\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>argocd app get shop-api --refresh | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -6\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Sync Status: OutOfSync\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Sync Policy: Automated (Prune)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>argocd app diff shop-api | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">===== apps/Deployment shop/api ======\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">&lt; (deleted in source)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The path in the Application no longer matched the folder, so the render was empty and prune removed everything. The team restored the path, resynced, then set Prune=false on stateful resources and added a pre-merge check that renders the manifests.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Turning on prune everywhere on day one\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Reading only sync status and ignoring health\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Fighting controllers instead of ignoring fields\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No review for repository restructures\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"two-statuses","text":"Two statuses"},{"id":"manual-or-automated","text":"Manual or automated"},{"id":"why-prune-needs-care","text":"Why prune needs care"},{"id":"expected-differences","text":"Expected differences"},{"id":"retrying","text":"Retrying"},{"id":"incident-the-prune-that-removed-a-namespace","text":"Incident: The prune that removed a namespace"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Automated sync policy","u":"https://argo-cd.readthedocs.io/en/stable/user-guide/auto_sync/"},{"t":"Argo CD documentation","u":"https://argo-cd.readthedocs.io/en/stable/"}],"text":"Argo CD shows two separate statuses for every application. Sync status says whether live state matches the repository. Health status says whether the resources actually work. They are independent, and reading them together tells you what to do next. Automation options such as prune and self-heal are powerful and need care. What you will learn Read Synced, OutOfSync, Healthy, Progressing and Degraded Choose a sync policy Understand prune and self-heal Use sync options and ignore rules for expected differences Two statuses Sync status: Synced (live matches desired), OutOfSync (they differ) or Unknown (cannot compare). Health: Healthy, Progressing (rolling out), Degraded (failing), Suspended, Missing. An application can be Synced but Degraded: the right manifests are applied but the pods crash. It can be OutOfSync but Healthy: someone changed a replica count and the app still works. Manual or automated With manual sync, Argo CD shows differences and waits for a person or pipeline to sync. With automated sync, it applies new commits on its own. Two further switches matter. Prune deletes resources that are no longer in the repository. Self-heal reverts live changes made outside the repository. syncPolicy: automated: prune: true selfHeal: true Use automation for non-production first. For production, many teams automate but keep prune off until they trust their repository layout, or require a manual sync for risky applications. Why prune needs care Prune removes anything Argo CD manages that disappears from the source. A renamed folder, a wrong path or an empty render can look like \"delete everything\". Protect critical resources with the annotation argocd.argoproj.io/sync-options: Prune=false , and use sync windows or manual approval for sensitive applications. Expected differences Controllers add fields, such as an autoscaler changing replicas or a webhook injecting certificates. Tell Argo CD to ignore them with ignoreDifferences for those paths, or the application will show OutOfSync forever. Sync options like ServerSideApply=true help when several controllers manage one object. Retrying A sync can fail on a bad manifest. Use retry settings for transient problems, and read the sync result message for permanent ones. Argo CD records sync history so you can see what was applied at each revision. Predict first. An app is Synced but Degraded. What does that tell you? Your guess Answer: The manifests match the repository, but the workload is failing; look at pod events and logs, not at the diff. Separate questions. Incident: The prune that removed a namespace Incident After a folder rename, an automated application with prune turned on deleted its resources. $ argocd app get shop-api --refresh | head -6 Sync Status: OutOfSync Sync Policy: Automated (Prune) $ argocd app diff shop-api | head -3 ===== apps/Deployment shop/api ====== \u003c (deleted in source) The path in the Application no longer matched the folder, so the render was empty and prune removed everything. The team restored the path, resynced, then set Prune=false on stateful resources and added a pre-merge check that renders the manifests. Common mistakes Turning on prune everywhere on day one Reading only sync status and ignoring health Fighting controllers instead of ignoring fields No review for repository restructures"},"argocd/helm-and-kustomize-with-argo-cd":{"html":"\u003cp>Most real repositories use Helm charts, Kustomize overlays or both. Argo CD renders them itself and applies the result as plain manifests. This has consequences: Helm hooks and release records work differently, and values must live in the repository to stay reviewable.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Point an Application at a Helm chart or a Kustomize folder\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Set values per environment\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Know how Argo CD treats Helm releases\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Pin chart versions\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"helm-sources\">Helm sources\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The Application source can be a chart in a Helm repository or a folder in the repository. Values come from a values file or inline parameters.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"source:\n  repoURL: https://charts.example.com\n  chart: shop-api\n  targetRevision: 1.4.2\n  helm:\n    valueFiles:\n      - values-prod.yaml\n    parameters:\n      - name: replicaCount\n        value: &quot;4&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">source:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">repoURL:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">https://charts.example.com\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">chart:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop-api\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">targetRevision:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">1.4\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">.2\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">helm:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">valueFiles:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">values-prod.yaml\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">parameters:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">replicaCount\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">value:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;4&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Argo CD runs the equivalent of \u003ccode>helm template\u003c/code> and applies the output. It does not run \u003ccode>helm install\u003c/code>, so \u003ccode>helm list\u003c/code> will not show the release and Helm release history is not used. Argo CD tracks history itself.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"kustomize\">Kustomize\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Point at a folder with a \u003ccode>kustomization.yaml\u003c/code>. An overlay per environment patches a shared base: change replicas, resource limits, image tags and config. \u003ccode>kustomize edit set image\u003c/code> or the Application&#39;s \u003ccode>kustomize.images\u003c/code> field updates the image.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"keep-values-in-the-repository\">Keep values in the repository\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Inline overrides set in the UI become live state that nobody reviewed. Prefer values files committed to the repository, one per environment, so a diff in a pull request shows exactly what changes in production.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"pin-versions\">Pin versions\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Use an exact chart version, not a floating range or \u003ccode>HEAD\u003c/code>. Update by a pull request that shows the rendered difference. Tools that render manifests in the pull request make review easy.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"hooks-and-tests\">Hooks and tests\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Helm hooks are converted into Argo CD hooks where possible, but behaviour differs from Helm&#39;s release lifecycle. Test the render locally with \u003ccode>helm template\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>kustomize build\u003c/code> and compare with \u003ccode>argocd app diff\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> Why does helm list not show a release deployed by Argo CD?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Argo CD renders the chart and applies the manifests itself, without creating a Helm release.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Different tracking.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-value-nobody-reviewed\">Incident: The value nobody reviewed\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A parameter was changed in the UI to hotfix replicas, and a later commit silently reverted it.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"argocd app get shop-api -o yaml | grep -A3 parameters\ngrep replicaCount apps/shop-api/values-prod.yaml\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>argocd app get shop-api -o yaml | grep -A3 parameters\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">    parameters:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">    - name: replicaCount\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">      value: &quot;8&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>grep replicaCount apps/shop-api/values-prod.yaml\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">replicaCount: 4\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>UI parameters override files, so the hotfix existed only in the application object. The team moved the value into the file, removed the override, and made the UI read-only for parameters on production.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Overrides outside the repository\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Floating chart versions\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Assuming Helm release commands work\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Not rendering locally before merge\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"helm-sources","text":"Helm sources"},{"id":"kustomize","text":"Kustomize"},{"id":"keep-values-in-the-repository","text":"Keep values in the repository"},{"id":"pin-versions","text":"Pin versions"},{"id":"hooks-and-tests","text":"Hooks and tests"},{"id":"incident-the-value-nobody-reviewed","text":"Incident: The value nobody reviewed"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Helm in Argo CD","u":"https://argo-cd.readthedocs.io/en/stable/user-guide/helm/"},{"t":"Kustomize in Argo CD","u":"https://argo-cd.readthedocs.io/en/stable/user-guide/kustomize/"}],"text":"Most real repositories use Helm charts, Kustomize overlays or both. Argo CD renders them itself and applies the result as plain manifests. This has consequences: Helm hooks and release records work differently, and values must live in the repository to stay reviewable. What you will learn Point an Application at a Helm chart or a Kustomize folder Set values per environment Know how Argo CD treats Helm releases Pin chart versions Helm sources The Application source can be a chart in a Helm repository or a folder in the repository. Values come from a values file or inline parameters. source: repoURL: https://charts.example.com chart: shop-api targetRevision: 1.4 .2 helm: valueFiles: - values-prod.yaml parameters: - name: replicaCount value: \"4\" Argo CD runs the equivalent of helm template and applies the output. It does not run helm install , so helm list will not show the release and Helm release history is not used. Argo CD tracks history itself. Kustomize Point at a folder with a kustomization.yaml . An overlay per environment patches a shared base: change replicas, resource limits, image tags and config. kustomize edit set image or the Application&#39;s kustomize.images field updates the image. Keep values in the repository Inline overrides set in the UI become live state that nobody reviewed. Prefer values files committed to the repository, one per environment, so a diff in a pull request shows exactly what changes in production. Pin versions Use an exact chart version, not a floating range or HEAD . Update by a pull request that shows the rendered difference. Tools that render manifests in the pull request make review easy. Hooks and tests Helm hooks are converted into Argo CD hooks where possible, but behaviour differs from Helm&#39;s release lifecycle. Test the render locally with helm template or kustomize build and compare with argocd app diff . Predict first. Why does helm list not show a release deployed by Argo CD? Your guess Answer: Argo CD renders the chart and applies the manifests itself, without creating a Helm release. Different tracking. Incident: The value nobody reviewed Incident A parameter was changed in the UI to hotfix replicas, and a later commit silently reverted it. $ argocd app get shop-api -o yaml | grep -A3 parameters parameters: - name: replicaCount value: \"8\" $ grep replicaCount apps/shop-api/values-prod.yaml replicaCount: 4 UI parameters override files, so the hotfix existed only in the application object. The team moved the value into the file, removed the override, and made the UI read-only for parameters on production. Common mistakes Overrides outside the repository Floating chart versions Assuming Helm release commands work Not rendering locally before merge"},"argocd/environments-app-of-apps-and-applicationsets":{"html":"\u003cp>One application is easy. Fifty applications across dev, staging and production, and several clusters, need structure. The goal is that adding an application or an environment is a small, reviewable change, and promotion between environments is a commit.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Choose a repository layout\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Use the app-of-apps pattern\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Generate applications with ApplicationSets\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Promote changes between environments\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"layout\">Layout\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A common layout has a folder per application with a base and overlays per environment, or a folder per environment that points at shared charts. Keep application source code and configuration in separate repositories when possible, so a new image tag is a small commit that does not trigger application tests.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"app-of-apps\">App-of-apps\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A parent Application points at a folder full of Application manifests. Syncing the parent creates all the children. This lets you bootstrap a whole cluster from one command, and add a new application by adding one file.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"applicationsets\">ApplicationSets\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>An \u003cstrong>ApplicationSet\u003c/strong> generates Applications from a template and a generator. A list generator makes one per environment; a cluster generator makes one per registered cluster; a directory generator makes one per folder in the repository; a pull request generator can create temporary preview environments.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: argoproj.io/v1alpha1\nkind: ApplicationSet\nmetadata:\n  name: shop\n  namespace: argocd\nspec:\n  generators:\n    - list:\n        elements:\n          - env: staging\n          - env: production\n  template:\n    metadata:\n      name: &quot;shop-{{env}}&quot;\n    spec:\n      project: default\n      source:\n        repoURL: https://example.com/platform/shop-config.git\n        targetRevision: main\n        path: &quot;overlays/{{env}}&quot;\n      destination:\n        server: https://kubernetes.default.svc\n        namespace: &quot;shop-{{env}}&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">argoproj.io/v1alpha1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">ApplicationSet\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">namespace:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">argocd\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">generators:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">list:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">elements:\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">env:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">staging\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">env:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">production\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">template:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;shop-\u003cspan class=\"hljs-template-variable\">{{env}}\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">project:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">default\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">source:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">repoURL:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">https://example.com/platform/shop-config.git\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">targetRevision:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">main\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">path:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;overlays/\u003cspan class=\"hljs-template-variable\">{{env}}\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">destination:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">server:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">https://kubernetes.default.svc\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">namespace:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;shop-\u003cspan class=\"hljs-template-variable\">{{env}}\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"promotion\">Promotion\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Promote by copying a tested image tag or chart version from the staging overlay to production in a pull request, ideally automated after checks pass. Avoid promoting by branch merges that carry unrelated changes. Production should require review and a clear record.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"blast-radius\">Blast radius\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A generator that is mistaken can create or remove many applications at once. Use projects, review for ApplicationSet changes, and policies that limit deletion (for example, preserving resources when an application is removed).\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> You want one Application per cluster registered in Argo CD. Which feature fits?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> An ApplicationSet with a cluster generator.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Generated applications.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-fifty-applications-gone\">Incident: Fifty applications gone\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A change to an ApplicationSet filter matched nothing and the controller started removing the generated applications.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"argocd appset get shop | head -4\nargocd app list | wc -l\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>argocd appset get shop | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -4\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Name: shop\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Generators: git directories: apps/*\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>argocd app list | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">wc\u003c/span> -l\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">2\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A folder rename left the generator with no matches. The team restored the path, set the policy that keeps generated applications when the set changes, and required a second reviewer on ApplicationSet files.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>One giant repository folder with no structure\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Copy-pasted Application files\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Promoting with merges that carry other changes\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No review on generators\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"layout","text":"Layout"},{"id":"app-of-apps","text":"App-of-apps"},{"id":"applicationsets","text":"ApplicationSets"},{"id":"promotion","text":"Promotion"},{"id":"blast-radius","text":"Blast radius"},{"id":"incident-fifty-applications-gone","text":"Incident: Fifty applications gone"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"ApplicationSet controller","u":"https://argo-cd.readthedocs.io/en/stable/operator-manual/applicationset/"},{"t":"Cluster bootstrapping","u":"https://argo-cd.readthedocs.io/en/stable/operator-manual/cluster-bootstrapping/"}],"text":"One application is easy. Fifty applications across dev, staging and production, and several clusters, need structure. The goal is that adding an application or an environment is a small, reviewable change, and promotion between environments is a commit. What you will learn Choose a repository layout Use the app-of-apps pattern Generate applications with ApplicationSets Promote changes between environments Layout A common layout has a folder per application with a base and overlays per environment, or a folder per environment that points at shared charts. Keep application source code and configuration in separate repositories when possible, so a new image tag is a small commit that does not trigger application tests. App-of-apps A parent Application points at a folder full of Application manifests. Syncing the parent creates all the children. This lets you bootstrap a whole cluster from one command, and add a new application by adding one file. ApplicationSets An ApplicationSet generates Applications from a template and a generator. A list generator makes one per environment; a cluster generator makes one per registered cluster; a directory generator makes one per folder in the repository; a pull request generator can create temporary preview environments. apiVersion: argoproj.io/v1alpha1 kind: ApplicationSet metadata: name: shop namespace: argocd spec: generators: - list: elements: - env: staging - env: production template: metadata: name: \"shop- {{env}} \" spec: project: default source: repoURL: https://example.com/platform/shop-config.git targetRevision: main path: \"overlays/ {{env}} \" destination: server: https://kubernetes.default.svc namespace: \"shop- {{env}} \" Promotion Promote by copying a tested image tag or chart version from the staging overlay to production in a pull request, ideally automated after checks pass. Avoid promoting by branch merges that carry unrelated changes. Production should require review and a clear record. Blast radius A generator that is mistaken can create or remove many applications at once. Use projects, review for ApplicationSet changes, and policies that limit deletion (for example, preserving resources when an application is removed). Predict first. You want one Application per cluster registered in Argo CD. Which feature fits? Your guess Answer: An ApplicationSet with a cluster generator. Generated applications. Incident: Fifty applications gone Incident A change to an ApplicationSet filter matched nothing and the controller started removing the generated applications. $ argocd appset get shop | head -4 Name: shop Generators: git directories: apps/* $ argocd app list | wc -l 2 A folder rename left the generator with no matches. The team restored the path, set the policy that keeps generated applications when the set changes, and required a second reviewer on ApplicationSet files. Common mistakes One giant repository folder with no structure Copy-pasted Application files Promoting with merges that carry other changes No review on generators"},"argocd/hooks-sync-waves-and-progressive-delivery":{"html":"\u003cp>Real releases have order: create the namespace and the database schema before the application, run a migration job before pods start, smoke-test afterwards. Argo CD provides hooks and sync waves for this, and a companion project, Argo Rollouts, adds canary and blue-green rollouts with automated analysis.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Order resources with sync waves\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Run jobs with hooks\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Describe canary and blue-green strategies\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Explain how analysis can stop a bad rollout\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"sync-waves\">Sync waves\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Annotate resources with \u003ccode>argocd.argoproj.io/sync-wave: &quot;N&quot;\u003c/code>. Lower numbers sync first, and Argo CD waits for each wave to be healthy before starting the next. Default is wave 0. Use negative waves for namespaces and CRDs, and higher ones for things that depend on the application.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"hooks\">Hooks\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A hook is a resource, usually a Job, annotated with \u003ccode>argocd.argoproj.io/hook\u003c/code>. \u003cstrong>PreSync\u003c/strong> runs before the main sync (database migration), \u003cstrong>Sync\u003c/strong> during it, \u003cstrong>PostSync\u003c/strong> afterwards (smoke test), \u003cstrong>SyncFail\u003c/strong> when it fails. Set \u003ccode>hook-delete-policy\u003c/code> such as \u003ccode>HookSucceeded\u003c/code> so completed jobs do not pile up. Make hooks idempotent, because syncs can be retried.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"canary-and-blue-green\">Canary and blue-green\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A plain Deployment does a rolling update with no view of user impact. \u003cstrong>Argo Rollouts\u003c/strong> replaces it with a Rollout resource. A \u003cstrong>canary\u003c/strong> sends a small share of traffic (5 percent, then 25, 50, 100) to the new version with pauses. \u003cstrong>Blue-green\u003c/strong> runs the new version beside the old and switches traffic at once after checks, keeping the old for fast rollback. Traffic splitting uses an ingress controller or a service mesh.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"automated-analysis\">Automated analysis\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>An AnalysisTemplate queries metrics, for example the error rate in Prometheus. If the new version breaks the threshold during a canary step, the rollout aborts and traffic returns to the stable version without a person. This ties delivery to your service level objectives.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"keep-it-simple-first\">Keep it simple first\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Start with sync waves and a PreSync migration. Add canary only when you have good metrics and enough traffic to see a signal in minutes.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A migration Job must run before the new pods start. Which Argo CD feature fits?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> A PreSync hook on the Job.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Hooks run around the sync.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-migration-that-ran-twice\">Incident: The migration that ran twice\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A retried sync ran a PreSync migration a second time and failed on a duplicate column.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl logs job/migrate -n shop\nkubectl get job migrate -n shop -o yaml | grep hook\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl logs job/migrate -n shop\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">ERROR: column &quot;currency&quot; of relation &quot;orders&quot; already exists\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get job migrate -n shop -o yaml | grep hook\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">argocd.argoproj.io/hook: PreSync\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The migration was not idempotent. The team rewrote it to check for the column first, recorded versions in a migrations table, and set the hook delete policy so each run starts clean.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Non-idempotent hooks\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Waves that deadlock on health\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Canary without metrics\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No rollback plan for data changes\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"sync-waves","text":"Sync waves"},{"id":"hooks","text":"Hooks"},{"id":"canary-and-blue-green","text":"Canary and blue-green"},{"id":"automated-analysis","text":"Automated analysis"},{"id":"keep-it-simple-first","text":"Keep it simple first"},{"id":"incident-the-migration-that-ran-twice","text":"Incident: The migration that ran twice"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Sync phases and waves","u":"https://argo-cd.readthedocs.io/en/stable/user-guide/sync-waves/"},{"t":"Resource hooks","u":"https://argo-cd.readthedocs.io/en/stable/user-guide/resource_hooks/"}],"text":"Real releases have order: create the namespace and the database schema before the application, run a migration job before pods start, smoke-test afterwards. Argo CD provides hooks and sync waves for this, and a companion project, Argo Rollouts, adds canary and blue-green rollouts with automated analysis. What you will learn Order resources with sync waves Run jobs with hooks Describe canary and blue-green strategies Explain how analysis can stop a bad rollout Sync waves Annotate resources with argocd.argoproj.io/sync-wave: \"N\" . Lower numbers sync first, and Argo CD waits for each wave to be healthy before starting the next. Default is wave 0. Use negative waves for namespaces and CRDs, and higher ones for things that depend on the application. Hooks A hook is a resource, usually a Job, annotated with argocd.argoproj.io/hook . PreSync runs before the main sync (database migration), Sync during it, PostSync afterwards (smoke test), SyncFail when it fails. Set hook-delete-policy such as HookSucceeded so completed jobs do not pile up. Make hooks idempotent, because syncs can be retried. Canary and blue-green A plain Deployment does a rolling update with no view of user impact. Argo Rollouts replaces it with a Rollout resource. A canary sends a small share of traffic (5 percent, then 25, 50, 100) to the new version with pauses. Blue-green runs the new version beside the old and switches traffic at once after checks, keeping the old for fast rollback. Traffic splitting uses an ingress controller or a service mesh. Automated analysis An AnalysisTemplate queries metrics, for example the error rate in Prometheus. If the new version breaks the threshold during a canary step, the rollout aborts and traffic returns to the stable version without a person. This ties delivery to your service level objectives. Keep it simple first Start with sync waves and a PreSync migration. Add canary only when you have good metrics and enough traffic to see a signal in minutes. Predict first. A migration Job must run before the new pods start. Which Argo CD feature fits? Your guess Answer: A PreSync hook on the Job. Hooks run around the sync. Incident: The migration that ran twice Incident A retried sync ran a PreSync migration a second time and failed on a duplicate column. $ kubectl logs job/migrate -n shop ERROR: column \"currency\" of relation \"orders\" already exists $ kubectl get job migrate -n shop -o yaml | grep hook argocd.argoproj.io/hook: PreSync The migration was not idempotent. The team rewrote it to check for the column first, recorded versions in a migrations table, and set the hook delete policy so each run starts clean. Common mistakes Non-idempotent hooks Waves that deadlock on health Canary without metrics No rollback plan for data changes"},"argocd/access-projects-secrets-and-multi-cluster":{"html":"\u003cp>Argo CD is powerful: it can create anything in the clusters it manages, so protecting it matters. Three topics decide whether a shared installation is safe: who may do what (projects, RBAC and single sign-on), where secrets live, and how remote clusters are registered.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Use projects to limit sources, destinations and kinds\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Configure RBAC and single sign-on\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Handle secrets without storing them in plain text\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Register and secure multiple clusters\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"projects\">Projects\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>An \u003cstrong>AppProject\u003c/strong> limits which repositories an application may use, which clusters and namespaces it may deploy to, and which resource kinds it may create (for example, no cluster roles). Give each team a project. Do not run everything in \u003ccode>default\u003c/code>, which is unrestricted.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: argoproj.io/v1alpha1\nkind: AppProject\nmetadata:\n  name: team-shop\n  namespace: argocd\nspec:\n  sourceRepos: [&quot;https://example.com/platform/shop-config.git&quot;]\n  destinations:\n    - server: https://kubernetes.default.svc\n      namespace: &quot;shop-*&quot;\n  clusterResourceWhitelist: []\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">argoproj.io/v1alpha1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">AppProject\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">team-shop\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">namespace:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">argocd\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">sourceRepos:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;https://example.com/platform/shop-config.git&quot;\u003c/span>]\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">destinations:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">server:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">https://kubernetes.default.svc\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">namespace:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;shop-*&quot;\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">clusterResourceWhitelist:\u003c/span> []\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"rbac-and-sign-in\">RBAC and sign-in\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Connect single sign-on (OpenID Connect) so people use company identities and MFA, and disable the shared admin account. Map groups to roles in \u003ccode>argocd-rbac-cm\u003c/code>: viewers see, developers sync their project, platform administrators manage projects. Use project roles and tokens for automation with limited scope.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"secrets\">Secrets\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The repository must not hold plain secrets. Common approaches: \u003cstrong>sealed or encrypted secrets\u003c/strong> committed in encrypted form, \u003cstrong>External Secrets Operator\u003c/strong> that creates Kubernetes Secrets from a cloud secrets manager, or a \u003cstrong>plugin\u003c/strong> that injects values at render time. Choose one and standardise it. Remember that Argo CD itself stores cluster credentials and repository credentials as secrets, so protect its namespace.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"multiple-clusters\">Multiple clusters\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Register a remote cluster with \u003ccode>argocd cluster add\u003c/code>, which creates a service account in that cluster and stores its token in Argo CD. Prefer a hub model with one control cluster and narrow roles per target, or one Argo CD per environment tier so a compromise of non-production cannot reach production. Limit what the remote service account can do where possible.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> Teams share one Argo CD. How do you stop team A deploying into team B's namespaces?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Give each team an AppProject with its own allowed destinations and sources, and bind roles to that project.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Projects are the boundary.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-secret-in-the-repository\">Incident: Secret in the repository\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A scan found a database password in a values file inside the configuration repository.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"grep -rn &quot;password:&quot; apps/shop-api/values-prod.yaml\ngit log --oneline -- apps/shop-api/values-prod.yaml | wc -l\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>grep -rn \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;password:&quot;\u003c/span> apps/shop-api/values-prod.yaml\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">12:  password: &quot;Pr0d-Db-2024!&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>git \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">log\u003c/span> --oneline -- apps/shop-api/values-prod.yaml | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">wc\u003c/span> -l\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">9\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The password existed in nine commits. The team rotated it first, since history cannot be trusted to hide it, then replaced the value with a reference to the External Secrets Operator, added secret scanning in pull requests, and restricted access to the repository.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Using the default project for everything\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Shared admin login\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Plain secrets in values files\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>One Argo CD with unlimited reach into production\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"projects","text":"Projects"},{"id":"rbac-and-sign-in","text":"RBAC and sign-in"},{"id":"secrets","text":"Secrets"},{"id":"multiple-clusters","text":"Multiple clusters"},{"id":"incident-secret-in-the-repository","text":"Incident: Secret in the repository"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Argo CD security","u":"https://argo-cd.readthedocs.io/en/stable/operator-manual/security/"},{"t":"Projects","u":"https://argo-cd.readthedocs.io/en/stable/user-guide/projects/"}],"text":"Argo CD is powerful: it can create anything in the clusters it manages, so protecting it matters. Three topics decide whether a shared installation is safe: who may do what (projects, RBAC and single sign-on), where secrets live, and how remote clusters are registered. What you will learn Use projects to limit sources, destinations and kinds Configure RBAC and single sign-on Handle secrets without storing them in plain text Register and secure multiple clusters Projects An AppProject limits which repositories an application may use, which clusters and namespaces it may deploy to, and which resource kinds it may create (for example, no cluster roles). Give each team a project. Do not run everything in default , which is unrestricted. apiVersion: argoproj.io/v1alpha1 kind: AppProject metadata: name: team-shop namespace: argocd spec: sourceRepos: [ \"https://example.com/platform/shop-config.git\" ] destinations: - server: https://kubernetes.default.svc namespace: \"shop-*\" clusterResourceWhitelist: [] RBAC and sign-in Connect single sign-on (OpenID Connect) so people use company identities and MFA, and disable the shared admin account. Map groups to roles in argocd-rbac-cm : viewers see, developers sync their project, platform administrators manage projects. Use project roles and tokens for automation with limited scope. Secrets The repository must not hold plain secrets. Common approaches: sealed or encrypted secrets committed in encrypted form, External Secrets Operator that creates Kubernetes Secrets from a cloud secrets manager, or a plugin that injects values at render time. Choose one and standardise it. Remember that Argo CD itself stores cluster credentials and repository credentials as secrets, so protect its namespace. Multiple clusters Register a remote cluster with argocd cluster add , which creates a service account in that cluster and stores its token in Argo CD. Prefer a hub model with one control cluster and narrow roles per target, or one Argo CD per environment tier so a compromise of non-production cannot reach production. Limit what the remote service account can do where possible. Predict first. Teams share one Argo CD. How do you stop team A deploying into team B's namespaces? Your guess Answer: Give each team an AppProject with its own allowed destinations and sources, and bind roles to that project. Projects are the boundary. Incident: Secret in the repository Incident A scan found a database password in a values file inside the configuration repository. $ grep -rn \"password:\" apps/shop-api/values-prod.yaml 12: password: \"Pr0d-Db-2024!\" $ git log --oneline -- apps/shop-api/values-prod.yaml | wc -l 9 The password existed in nine commits. The team rotated it first, since history cannot be trusted to hide it, then replaced the value with a reference to the External Secrets Operator, added secret scanning in pull requests, and restricted access to the repository. Common mistakes Using the default project for everything Shared admin login Plain secrets in values files One Argo CD with unlimited reach into production"},"argocd/debugging-argo-cd":{"html":"\u003cp>Argo CD problems look like a handful of patterns: it cannot read the repository, it cannot render the manifests, it cannot apply them, or the applied resources are unhealthy. Walking those four stages in order finds almost every cause.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Diagnose repository and credential errors\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Diagnose render failures\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Diagnose apply and permission failures\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Diagnose health problems and endless OutOfSync\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"four-stages\">Four stages\u003c/h2>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Fetch:\u003c/strong> can the repo server clone the repository and find the path and revision? 2. \u003cstrong>Render:\u003c/strong> does Helm or Kustomize produce valid manifests with the given values? 3. \u003cstrong>Apply:\u003c/strong> are the manifests accepted by the API server, and does the destination allow them? 4. \u003cstrong>Health:\u003c/strong> do the created resources become healthy? \u003ccode>argocd app get NAME\u003c/code> shows conditions for the first failing stage.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003ch2 id=\"fetch-and-render\">Fetch and render\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Errors like &quot;authentication required&quot;, &quot;revision not found&quot; or &quot;app path does not exist&quot; are fetch problems: check repository credentials, branch names and the folder. Render errors show the Helm or Kustomize message (&quot;nil pointer evaluating&quot;, &quot;unable to find one &#39;resource&#39;&quot;): reproduce locally with \u003ccode>helm template\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>kustomize build\u003c/code> using the same values. Check that the repo server has network access to chart repositories.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"apply-failures\">Apply failures\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>&quot;is forbidden&quot; means the Argo CD service account lacks permission; &quot;not permitted in project&quot; means the project does not allow that kind or destination; webhook or admission errors come from the cluster; &quot;immutable field&quot; needs the resource to be replaced; &quot;CRD not found&quot; means ordering: sync the CRD first using a wave. Use \u003ccode>argocd app sync NAME --dry-run\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>argocd app diff\u003c/code> to see what would be applied.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"health-and-outofsync-loops\">Health and OutOfSync loops\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A Degraded application needs ordinary Kubernetes debugging on the resources: events, logs, probes. An application that is OutOfSync again right after a sync usually has another controller changing a field (add \u003ccode>ignoreDifferences\u003c/code>), a mutating webhook, or defaults added by the API server. Compare the live and desired YAML for the differing field.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"useful-commands\">Useful commands\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>argocd app get NAME\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>argocd app diff NAME\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>argocd app history NAME\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>argocd app logs NAME\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>kubectl -n argocd logs deploy/argocd-repo-server\u003c/code>, and \u003ccode>kubectl -n argocd logs statefulset/argocd-application-controller\u003c/code>. To roll back, revert the commit; the \u003ccode>argocd app rollback\u003c/code> command is limited when automatic sync is on.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> Sync fails with &quot;namespaces shop is forbidden&quot; in a project. What do you check?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Whether the project allows that destination namespace and whether the target service account has permission.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Project and RBAC.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-forever-outofsync\">Incident: Forever OutOfSync\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>An application showed OutOfSync after every sync although nothing had changed in the repository.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"argocd app diff shop-api\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>argocd app diff shop-api\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">===== apps/Deployment shop/api ======\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">  spec:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">    replicas:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">&lt;     2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">&gt;     5\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A horizontal autoscaler was setting replicas to 5 while the repository said 2. The team removed replicas from the manifest and added ignoreDifferences for that field, so each tool owned its own setting and the application stayed Synced.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Changing things before reading the condition\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Not reproducing the render locally\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Fighting autoscalers\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Forcing syncs with replace without understanding the cause\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"four-stages","text":"Four stages"},{"id":"fetch-and-render","text":"Fetch and render"},{"id":"apply-failures","text":"Apply failures"},{"id":"health-and-outofsync-loops","text":"Health and OutOfSync loops"},{"id":"useful-commands","text":"Useful commands"},{"id":"incident-forever-outofsync","text":"Incident: Forever OutOfSync"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Argo CD documentation","u":"https://argo-cd.readthedocs.io/en/stable/"},{"t":"Automated sync policy","u":"https://argo-cd.readthedocs.io/en/stable/user-guide/auto_sync/"}],"text":"Argo CD problems look like a handful of patterns: it cannot read the repository, it cannot render the manifests, it cannot apply them, or the applied resources are unhealthy. Walking those four stages in order finds almost every cause. What you will learn Diagnose repository and credential errors Diagnose render failures Diagnose apply and permission failures Diagnose health problems and endless OutOfSync Four stages Fetch: can the repo server clone the repository and find the path and revision? 2. Render: does Helm or Kustomize produce valid manifests with the given values? 3. Apply: are the manifests accepted by the API server, and does the destination allow them? 4. Health: do the created resources become healthy? argocd app get NAME shows conditions for the first failing stage. Fetch and render Errors like \"authentication required\", \"revision not found\" or \"app path does not exist\" are fetch problems: check repository credentials, branch names and the folder. Render errors show the Helm or Kustomize message (\"nil pointer evaluating\", \"unable to find one &#39;resource&#39;\"): reproduce locally with helm template or kustomize build using the same values. Check that the repo server has network access to chart repositories. Apply failures \"is forbidden\" means the Argo CD service account lacks permission; \"not permitted in project\" means the project does not allow that kind or destination; webhook or admission errors come from the cluster; \"immutable field\" needs the resource to be replaced; \"CRD not found\" means ordering: sync the CRD first using a wave. Use argocd app sync NAME --dry-run and argocd app diff to see what would be applied. Health and OutOfSync loops A Degraded application needs ordinary Kubernetes debugging on the resources: events, logs, probes. An application that is OutOfSync again right after a sync usually has another controller changing a field (add ignoreDifferences ), a mutating webhook, or defaults added by the API server. Compare the live and desired YAML for the differing field. Useful commands argocd app get NAME , argocd app diff NAME , argocd app history NAME , argocd app logs NAME , kubectl -n argocd logs deploy/argocd-repo-server , and kubectl -n argocd logs statefulset/argocd-application-controller . To roll back, revert the commit; the argocd app rollback command is limited when automatic sync is on. Predict first. Sync fails with \"namespaces shop is forbidden\" in a project. What do you check? Your guess Answer: Whether the project allows that destination namespace and whether the target service account has permission. Project and RBAC. Incident: Forever OutOfSync Incident An application showed OutOfSync after every sync although nothing had changed in the repository. $ argocd app diff shop-api ===== apps/Deployment shop/api ====== spec: replicas: \u003c 2 > 5 A horizontal autoscaler was setting replicas to 5 while the repository said 2. The team removed replicas from the manifest and added ignoreDifferences for that field, so each tool owned its own setting and the application stayed Synced. Common mistakes Changing things before reading the condition Not reproducing the render locally Fighting autoscalers Forcing syncs with replace without understanding the cause"}});